# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Title ##
#, no-wrap
msgid "Overview of error mitigation techniques {#overview-of-error-mitigation-techniques}"
msgstr "Gambaran umum teknik error mitigation {#overview-of-error-mitigation-techniques}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Error mitigation refers to any process that attempts to reduce the effects of errors in quantum computations after they have occurred. It is different from suppression, which attempts to prevent errors, and from error correction, which encodes information across many physical qubits and checks the consistency in that information to preserve information in a single logical qubit. Throughout this lesson, error mitigation techniques can best be understood as methods for constructing better estimators of ideal expectation values or better samplers of probability distributions, **not** as methods for correcting quantum states during execution. We will examine the error mitigation methods below, all of which are available in Qiskit and its addons, and which are promising tools in the pursuit of quantum advantage.\n"
msgstr "Error mitigation adalah proses apa pun yang berusaha mengurangi dampak error dalam komputasi kuantum setelah error itu terjadi. Ini berbeda dari suppression, yang berusaha mencegah error, dan dari error correction, yang mengodekan informasi ke banyak qubit fisik lalu memeriksa konsistensi informasi itu untuk menjaga informasi dalam satu qubit logis. Di sepanjang pelajaran ini, teknik error mitigation paling tepat dipahami sebagai metode untuk membangun estimator yang lebih baik bagi nilai ekspektasi ideal atau sampler yang lebih baik bagi distribusi probabilitas, **bukan** sebagai metode untuk mengoreksi keadaan kuantum saat eksekusi. Kita akan membahas metode error mitigation di bawah, yang semuanya tersedia di Qiskit dan addon-nya, dan merupakan alat yang menjanjikan dalam upaya mencapai quantum advantage.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "**Twirled readout error extinction (TREX)** randomizes the measurement process by applying randomly selected Pauli operations before measurement and then classically undoing their known effects on the observed bitstrings. Under suitable measurement-noise assumptions, this converts the readout error channel into a simpler and more symmetric form that is easier to calibrate and mitigate.\n"
msgstr "**Twirled readout error extinction (TREX)** mengacak proses pengukuran dengan menerapkan operasi Pauli yang dipilih secara acak sebelum pengukuran, lalu secara klasik membatalkan efek yang diketahui dari operasi itu pada bitstring yang teramati. Di bawah asumsi derau pengukuran yang sesuai, ini mengubah kanal error readout menjadi bentuk yang lebih sederhana dan simetris sehingga lebih mudah dikalibrasi dan dimitigasi.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "**Matrix-free measurement mitigation (M3)** is a scalable measurement-error mitigation technique that avoids explicitly constructing the assignment matrix or its inverse.\n"
msgstr "**Matrix-free measurement mitigation (M3)** adalah teknik measurement-error mitigation yang skalabel dan menghindari pembentukan matriks assignment atau inversnya secara eksplisit.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "**Zero noise extrapolation (ZNE)** approximates the value an observable would have in the absence of noise by artificially scaling noise by a known factor and extrapolating back to the zero-noise case. This requires that noise scale in a predictable way. That predictability might require other methods discussed in this lesson (pulse stretching, gate folding, and probabilistic error amplification (PEA)).\n"
msgstr "**Zero noise extrapolation (ZNE)** memperkirakan nilai yang akan dimiliki sebuah observable tanpa derau dengan cara menskalakan derau secara artifisial dengan faktor yang diketahui lalu mengekstrapolasi kembali ke kasus tanpa derau. Ini mensyaratkan derau berskala dengan cara yang dapat diprediksi. Prediktabilitas itu mungkin membutuhkan metode lain yang dibahas dalam pelajaran ini (pulse stretching, gate folding, dan probabilistic error amplification (PEA)).\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "**Probabilistic error cancellation (PEC)** is among the most powerful, but also the most computationally expensive, error-mitigation techniques. It works by learning the noise behavior of the system and attempting to undo the noise by statistically constructing an unbiased estimate of the ideal computation. PEC outputs statistically corrected observables, not error-corrected results.\n"
msgstr "**Probabilistic error cancellation (PEC)** termasuk teknik error mitigation yang paling andal, tetapi juga paling mahal secara komputasi. Teknik ini bekerja dengan mempelajari perilaku derau sistem dan berusaha membatalkan derau dengan membangun estimasi tak bias dari komputasi ideal secara statistik. PEC menghasilkan observable yang dikoreksi secara statistik, bukan hasil yang dikoreksi error.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "We will now dive into each of these methods.\n"
msgstr "Sekarang kita akan mendalami masing-masing metode ini.\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Title ##
#, no-wrap
msgid "M3 {#m3}"
msgstr "M3 {#m3}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Measurement errors are one of the most persistent and hardware-specific sources of noise in near-term quantum devices. Even when a quantum state is prepared and evolved correctly, the final readout step can misreport the true computational basis outcome. These errors arise from imperfect discrimination between the physical states corresponding to $|0\\rangle$ and $|1\\rangle$, relaxation during measurement, and imperfections in measurement electronics. As a result, the observed distribution of bitstrings is often a distorted version of the true (ideal) distribution, with probability incorrectly reassigned between outcomes.\n"
msgstr "Error pengukuran adalah salah satu sumber derau yang paling persisten dan spesifik hardware pada perangkat kuantum jangka dekat. Bahkan ketika keadaan kuantum disiapkan dan berevolusi dengan benar, langkah readout akhir bisa salah melaporkan hasil basis komputasi yang sebenarnya. Error ini muncul dari pembedaan yang tidak sempurna antara keadaan fisik yang bersesuaian dengan $|0\\rangle$ dan $|1\\rangle$, relaksasi selama pengukuran, dan ketidaksempurnaan elektronik pengukuran. Akibatnya, distribusi bitstring yang teramati sering kali merupakan versi terdistorsi dari distribusi sebenarnya (ideal), dengan probabilitas yang salah dialihkan antar hasil.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Measurement mitigation addresses this problem by explicitly modeling how measurement outcomes are misassigned. In the standard formulation, this behavior is captured by an assignment matrix $A$, whose elements $A_{ij}$ represent the probability of recording outcome $i$ when the true basis state was $j$. If the measurement noise is classical and stationary, the observed probability vector $p_{\\text{obs}}$ is related to the ideal distribution $p_{\\text{ideal}}$ by a simple linear relation,\n"
"$$\n"
"p_{\\text{obs}} = A\\, p_{\\text{ideal}}.\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"Measurement mitigation mengatasi masalah ini dengan memodelkan secara eksplisit bagaimana hasil pengukuran salah ditetapkan. Dalam formulasi standar, perilaku ini ditangkap oleh matriks assignment $A$, yang elemen $A_{ij}$-nya menyatakan probabilitas mencatat hasil $i$ ketika keadaan basis sebenarnya adalah $j$. Jika derau pengukuran bersifat klasik dan stasioner, vektor probabilitas teramati $p_{\\text{obs}}$ berhubungan dengan distribusi ideal $p_{\\text{ideal}}$ melalui relasi linear sederhana,\n"
"$$\n"
"p_{\\text{obs}} = A\\, p_{\\text{ideal}}.\n"
"$$\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Note that the assignment matrix $A$ is a classical stochastic map, not a quantum channel. It acts on classical readouts, not on the underlying quantum states that were sampled to obtain the readouts.\n"
msgstr "Perhatikan bahwa matriks assignment $A$ adalah peta stokastik klasik, bukan kanal kuantum. Matriks ini bekerja pada hasil readout klasik, bukan pada keadaan kuantum yang disampel untuk mendapatkan readout tersebut.\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![A histogram of many measurements. Two matrices are also shown. One is a full assignment matrix describing all possible measurements. The other is a reduced matrix functioning as an assignment matrix only in the subspace of measured states.](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/error-mitigation/m3-counts.avif)\n"
msgstr "![A histogram of many measurements. Two matrices are also shown. One is a full assignment matrix describing all possible measurements. The other is a reduced matrix functioning as an assignment matrix only in the subspace of measured states.](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/error-mitigation/m3-counts.avif)\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Matrix-free measurement mitigation (M3) uses this structure to construct a mitigated estimate of $p_{\\text{ideal}}$ without ever forming or inverting the full matrix $A$, which would be infeasible for large numbers of qubits. Instead, M3 exploits the fact that many near-term quantum workloads produce highly peaked or low-entropy distributions, so that mitigation focuses only on the subset of outcomes that significantly contribute to the observed distribution.\n"
msgstr "Matrix-free measurement mitigation (M3) memakai struktur ini untuk membangun estimasi termitigasi dari $p_{\\text{ideal}}$ tanpa pernah membentuk atau membalik matriks $A$ penuh, yang tidak mungkin dilakukan untuk jumlah qubit yang besar. Sebaliknya, M3 memanfaatkan kenyataan bahwa banyak beban kerja kuantum jangka dekat menghasilkan distribusi yang sangat puncak atau berentropi rendah, sehingga mitigasi hanya berfokus pada subset hasil yang berkontribusi signifikan terhadap distribusi yang teramati.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "To build the assignment model, additional calibration experiments must be run on the quantum hardware. These typically involve preparing known computational basis states and recording how they are reported by the measurement system. The calibration overhead scales with the number of measured qubits, while the computational cost of mitigation depends on the structure of the observed distribution. This cost is far smaller than what would be required to characterize the full $2^n \\times 2^n$ assignment matrix explicitly. While this calibration overhead is real, it is lightweight compared to circuit-level techniques like zero-noise extrapolation or probabilistic error cancellation, making M3 a practical and widely used tool for mitigating readout errors in near-term quantum experiments.\n"
msgstr "Untuk membangun model assignment, eksperimen kalibrasi tambahan harus dijalankan di hardware kuantum. Biasanya ini melibatkan penyiapan keadaan basis komputasi yang diketahui dan pencatatan bagaimana keadaan itu dilaporkan oleh sistem pengukuran. Overhead kalibrasi berskala dengan jumlah qubit yang diukur, sedangkan biaya komputasi mitigasi bergantung pada struktur distribusi yang teramati. Biaya ini jauh lebih kecil daripada yang dibutuhkan untuk mengkarakterisasi matriks assignment $2^n \\times 2^n$ penuh secara eksplisit. Meski overhead kalibrasi ini nyata, ia ringan dibandingkan teknik tingkat circuit seperti zero-noise extrapolation atau probabilistic error cancellation, sehingga M3 menjadi alat praktis dan banyak dipakai untuk memitigasi error readout dalam eksperimen kuantum jangka dekat.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Note that calibration is only useful to the extent that the measurement process remains sufficiently stable between the calibration runs and the target experiment.\n"
msgstr "Perhatikan bahwa kalibrasi hanya berguna sejauh proses pengukuran tetap cukup stabil antara run kalibrasi dan eksperimen target.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "We will first carry out a simple case by hand to illustrate how it works, then use tools built into Qiskit to show the use of M3 at a more realistic scale.\n"
msgstr "Pertama kita akan mengerjakan kasus sederhana secara manual untuk mengilustrasikan cara kerjanya, lalu memakai alat bawaan Qiskit untuk menunjukkan penggunaan M3 pada skala yang lebih realistis.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "M3 small example {#m3-small-example}"
msgstr "Contoh kecil M3 {#m3-small-example}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "We start by building a simple Bell state.\n"
msgstr "Kita mulai dengan membangun keadaan Bell sederhana.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "We can use a simulator with a noise model because we are restricting this example to a very small circuit. We explicitly configure our noise model to have readout error of the form:\n"
msgstr "Kita bisa memakai simulator dengan model derau karena contoh ini dibatasi pada circuit yang sangat kecil. Kita mengonfigurasi model derau secara eksplisit agar memiliki error readout dengan bentuk berikut:\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "`if true=0: [95% chance of reading 0, 5% chance of reading 1]`\n"
msgstr "`if true=0: [95% chance of reading 0, 5% chance of reading 1]`\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "`if true=1: [8% chance of reading 0, 92% chance of reading 1]`\n"
msgstr "`if true=1: [8% chance of reading 0, 92% chance of reading 1]`\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "These readout error rates are much higher than what one sees in real IBM&reg; quantum computers. The high values have been chosen for pedagogical reasons, so that we can clearly see the effect of M3.\n"
msgstr "Tingkat error readout ini jauh lebih tinggi daripada yang terlihat pada komputer kuantum IBM&reg; sungguhan. Nilai tinggi ini dipilih demi tujuan pedagogis, supaya kita bisa melihat dengan jelas efek M3.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "We can run the circuit on the noisy simulator to observe the effects of readout error.\n"
msgstr "Kita bisa menjalankan circuit pada simulator berderau untuk mengamati efek error readout.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "In our toy example, **we defined the readout error** for our noise model. But when using a **real quantum computer**, the readout error is not known a priori. Instead, it must be estimated experimentally. For this, we create circuits to prepare a qubit in a known computational basis state $|0\\rangle$ or $|1\\rangle$ and measure it. In the absence of readout error we would measure exactly the prepared state. By collecting statistics from these measurements, we can build an assignment matrix describing the readout error. In this toy example, it should look similar to the one we defined.\n"
msgstr "Dalam contoh sederhana kita, **kita mendefinisikan sendiri error readout** untuk model derau. Namun saat memakai **komputer kuantum sungguhan**, error readout tidak diketahui sebelumnya. Error itu harus diestimasi secara eksperimental. Untuk itu, kita membuat circuit yang menyiapkan qubit dalam keadaan basis komputasi yang diketahui, $|0\\rangle$ atau $|1\\rangle$, lalu mengukurnya. Tanpa error readout, kita akan mengukur persis keadaan yang disiapkan. Dengan mengumpulkan statistik dari pengukuran ini, kita bisa membangun matriks assignment yang menggambarkan error readout. Dalam contoh sederhana ini, hasilnya seharusnya mirip dengan yang kita definisikan.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "For a more realistic system, statistics would need to be accumulated for each physical qubit. This is sometimes non-trivial, because transpilation maps an abstract circuit to hardware and the qubit number changes. We will revisit that in the next example. In this simulator example, we assigned the same readout-error model to every qubit, so a single characterization is sufficient.\n"
msgstr "Untuk sistem yang lebih realistis, statistik perlu dikumpulkan untuk setiap qubit fisik. Ini kadang tidak sepele, karena transpilasi memetakan circuit abstrak ke hardware dan nomor qubit berubah. Kita akan membahasnya lagi di contoh berikutnya. Dalam contoh simulator ini, kita menetapkan model error readout yang sama untuk setiap qubit, jadi satu karakterisasi sudah cukup.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Note that this is very close to the readout error matrix used to construct the simulator.\n"
msgstr "Perhatikan bahwa ini sangat dekat dengan matriks error readout yang dipakai untuk membangun simulator.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Again, on a real quantum computer we would have statistics like these for multiple physical qubits. For this simulator, we assume the two qubits have identical and independent readout errors, allowing us to construct the two-qubit assignment matrix `A_2q` as a tensor product of the corresponding one-qubit matrices. Also note that the point of M3 is **not** to construct the entire assignment matrix. Instead, M3 focuses on the subset of outcomes that significantly contribute to the observed distribution. We will build the entire matrix for our very small example to see how it works. The implementation of M3 in `qiskit-ibm-runtime` is more sophisticated than this toy example because it must handle many qubits with distinct readout characteristics while still avoiding construction of the full assignment matrix.\n"
msgstr "Sekali lagi, pada komputer kuantum sungguhan kita akan punya statistik seperti ini untuk beberapa qubit fisik. Untuk simulator ini, kita asumsikan kedua qubit memiliki error readout yang identik dan independen, sehingga kita bisa membangun matriks assignment dua-qubit `A_2q` sebagai hasil kali tensor dari matriks satu-qubit yang bersesuaian. Perhatikan juga bahwa tujuan M3 **bukan** membangun seluruh matriks assignment. Sebaliknya, M3 berfokus pada subset hasil yang berkontribusi signifikan terhadap distribusi yang teramati. Kita akan membangun seluruh matriks untuk contoh yang sangat kecil ini agar melihat cara kerjanya. Implementasi M3 di `qiskit-ibm-runtime` lebih canggih daripada contoh sederhana ini karena harus menangani banyak qubit dengan karakteristik readout yang berbeda-beda sambil tetap menghindari pembentukan matriks assignment penuh.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "For this example we assume that readout errors on different qubits are independent, allowing the two-qubit assignment matrix to be constructed as a tensor product of the corresponding one-qubit matrices.\n"
msgstr "Untuk contoh ini kita asumsikan error readout pada qubit yang berbeda saling independen, sehingga matriks assignment dua-qubit bisa dibangun sebagai hasil kali tensor dari matriks satu-qubit yang bersesuaian.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "We obtain the raw and mitigated results for comparison.\n"
msgstr "Kita mendapatkan hasil mentah dan hasil termitigasi untuk dibandingkan.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "At this point, we have experimentally characterized how the measurement process distorts ideal outcome probabilities. Our goal is now to undo this distortion and apply the resulting correction to the raw probabilities.\n"
msgstr "Sampai di sini, kita sudah mengkarakterisasi secara eksperimental bagaimana proses pengukuran mendistorsi probabilitas hasil yang ideal. Tujuan kita sekarang adalah membatalkan distorsi ini dan menerapkan koreksi yang dihasilkan pada probabilitas mentah.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Because we are performing a linear inversion rather than a physical correction, the resulting \"probabilities\" need not always be positive. Very small negative quasi-probabilities are expected and reflect the fact that measurement mitigation produces an estimator rather than a physical probability distribution.\n"
msgstr "Karena kita melakukan inversi linear, bukan koreksi fisik, \"probabilitas\" yang dihasilkan tidak selalu positif. Quasi-probabilitas negatif yang sangat kecil memang wajar dan mencerminkan fakta bahwa measurement mitigation menghasilkan sebuah estimator, bukan distribusi probabilitas fisik.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "We convert these to counts for plotting.\n"
msgstr "Kita ubah ini menjadi counts untuk diplot.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Finally we look at the raw, mitigated, and ideal distributions. The mitigated \"counts\" below are only a visualization aid obtained by rescaling the mitigated quasi-probabilities.\n"
msgstr "Terakhir kita lihat distribusi mentah, termitigasi, dan ideal. \"Counts\" termitigasi di bawah hanyalah alat bantu visualisasi yang diperoleh dengan menskalakan ulang quasi-probabilitas termitigasi.\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/error-mitigation/extracted-outputs/f10a0dda-0.avif)\n"
msgstr "![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/error-mitigation/extracted-outputs/f10a0dda-0.avif)\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Clearly, the M3-mitigated results are much closer to the ideal distributions, and we can see why.\n"
msgstr "Jelas bahwa hasil yang dimitigasi dengan M3 jauh lebih dekat ke distribusi ideal, dan kita bisa melihat alasannya.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Applying M3 to more realistic workflows is much more complex. Consider:\n"
"* Real quantum computers contain many physical qubits, each with its own readout-error characteristics. These errors must be modeled and combined appropriately.\n"
"* The full assignment matrix $A$ grows exponentially with the number of qubits and quickly becomes impractical to construct explicitly. This is why M3 focuses on the subset of outcomes that contribute significantly to the observed distribution rather than constructing the entire assignment matrix.\n"
"* For real problems of interest, the ideal distribution might not be known.\n"
msgstr ""
"Menerapkan M3 pada alur kerja yang lebih realistis jauh lebih kompleks. Pertimbangkan:\n"
"* Komputer kuantum sungguhan memiliki banyak qubit fisik, masing-masing dengan karakteristik error readout sendiri. Error ini harus dimodelkan dan digabungkan dengan tepat.\n"
"* Matriks assignment penuh $A$ tumbuh secara eksponensial terhadap jumlah qubit dan dengan cepat menjadi tidak praktis untuk dibangun secara eksplisit. Inilah sebabnya M3 berfokus pada subset hasil yang berkontribusi signifikan terhadap distribusi yang teramati, bukan membangun seluruh matriks assignment.\n"
"* Untuk masalah nyata yang menarik, distribusi ideal mungkin tidak diketahui.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "For all of these reasons, the full mitigation workflow is encapsulated in the `mthree` Qiskit addon.\n"
msgstr "Karena semua alasan ini, alur kerja mitigasi lengkap dikemas dalam addon Qiskit `mthree`.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Larger M3 example {#larger-m3-example}"
msgstr "Contoh M3 yang lebih besar {#larger-m3-example}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "We now apply M3 to a larger circuit, one for which determining the relevant subspace of $A$ by hand would be cumbersome, but which is still simple enough that we know the ideal distribution: a GHZ state. We start importing the `mthree` package and defining a GHZ quantum circuit.\n"
msgstr "Sekarang kita terapkan M3 pada circuit yang lebih besar, yang untuknya menentukan subruang relevan dari $A$ secara manual akan merepotkan, tetapi masih cukup sederhana sehingga kita tahu distribusi idealnya: keadaan GHZ. Kita mulai dengan mengimpor paket `mthree` dan mendefinisikan circuit kuantum GHZ.\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/error-mitigation/extracted-outputs/d7e4b190-0.avif)\n"
msgstr "![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/error-mitigation/extracted-outputs/d7e4b190-0.avif)\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "To run this on a real quantum computer, we import `qiskit_ibm_runtime` and other necessary packages. We use `SamplerV2` to obtain a distribution of outputs.\n"
msgstr "Untuk menjalankannya di komputer kuantum sungguhan, kita impor `qiskit_ibm_runtime` dan paket lain yang diperlukan. Kita memakai `SamplerV2` untuk mendapatkan distribusi keluaran.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Now we transpile the circuit to our backend. We are setting the optimization level to 0 here in order to focus on the effects of M3, without adding in additional tools. This means the final fidelity will not be representative of what is achievable on IBM Quantum&reg; hardware using the full suite of available tools, but it allows us to focus specifically on the usefulness of M3.\n"
msgstr "Sekarang kita transpile circuit ke backend kita. Kita menetapkan level optimisasi 0 di sini agar fokus pada efek M3, tanpa menambahkan alat tambahan. Artinya fidelity akhir tidak akan mewakili apa yang bisa dicapai di hardware IBM Quantum&reg; dengan seluruh rangkaian alat yang tersedia, tetapi ini memungkinkan kita fokus khusus pada kegunaan M3.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "We now configure the Sampler, again disabling additional error-suppression techniques such as dynamical decoupling and twirling.\n"
msgstr "Sekarang kita konfigurasi Sampler, lagi-lagi menonaktifkan teknik error-suppression tambahan seperti dynamical decoupling dan twirling.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Finally, we execute our job.\n"
msgstr "Terakhir, kita jalankan job kita.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "We can extract the raw counts from the circuit above.\n"
msgstr "Kita bisa mengekstrak counts mentah dari circuit di atas.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Now we run `mthree` on our backend to characterize the assignment model used for measurement mitigation. We rescale the raw distribution using that information. Note that this can sometimes result in very small negative probabilities (hence the term **quasi-probabilities**).\n"
msgstr "Sekarang kita jalankan `mthree` pada backend kita untuk mengkarakterisasi model assignment yang dipakai untuk measurement mitigation. Kita menskalakan ulang distribusi mentah memakai informasi itu. Perhatikan bahwa ini kadang menghasilkan probabilitas negatif yang sangat kecil (karena itu istilah **quasi-probabilitas**).\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The `mthree` correction produces quasi-probabilities, rather than counts. We can rescale to counts by hand. The helper function below converts to counts and rounds to integer values. Also, for large circuits, there will be many erroneous strings with very few counts, but which clutter a histogram. The second helper function below trims away any strings with fewer counts than some threshold we choose, to give us a clear view of the dominant terms in the distribution.\n"
msgstr "Koreksi `mthree` menghasilkan quasi-probabilitas, bukan counts. Kita bisa menskalakan ke counts secara manual. Fungsi pembantu di bawah mengubahnya menjadi counts dan membulatkan ke nilai bilangan bulat. Selain itu, untuk circuit besar akan ada banyak string keliru dengan counts sangat sedikit yang membuat histogram berantakan. Fungsi pembantu kedua di bawah memangkas string mana pun yang counts-nya di bawah ambang yang kita pilih, agar kita mendapat gambaran jelas tentang suku dominan dalam distribusi.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "We can also easily produce the theoretical, ideal distribution in which we have equal probabilities for the all-zero and all-one states, and zero probability for all other outcomes, for comparison.\n"
msgstr "Kita juga dengan mudah bisa menghasilkan distribusi ideal teoretis, yang memiliki probabilitas sama untuk keadaan semua-nol dan semua-satu, dan probabilitas nol untuk semua hasil lain, sebagai pembanding.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Finally, we can visualize our results.\n"
msgstr "Terakhir, kita bisa memvisualisasikan hasil kita.\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/error-mitigation/extracted-outputs/4a451fe6-0.avif)\n"
msgstr "![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/error-mitigation/extracted-outputs/4a451fe6-0.avif)\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The mitigated results are closer to the ideal distribution than the unmitigated results. We can quantify this better using counts for the ideal bitstrings.\n"
msgstr "Hasil termitigasi lebih dekat ke distribusi ideal daripada hasil tanpa mitigasi. Kita bisa mengkuantifikasi ini dengan lebih baik memakai counts untuk bitstring ideal.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "This simple tool improved our fidelity by 26% relative to the unmitigated fidelity, with fairly low overhead. Clearly M3 can be very valuable. But the GHZ circuit was selected as a good use case, because it is highly peaked on two output states. Some problems result in more evenly-distributed counts (high entropy states). For very deep circuits with many qubits, gate errors and noise unrelated to measurement errors will often result in a high-entropy state. In such cases, M3 operates on a state space that is quite large. There are three possible approaches to this:\n"
msgstr "Alat sederhana ini meningkatkan fidelity kita sebesar 26% relatif terhadap fidelity tanpa mitigasi, dengan overhead yang cukup rendah. Jelas M3 bisa sangat berharga. Namun circuit GHZ dipilih sebagai kasus penggunaan yang baik karena distribusinya sangat puncak pada dua keadaan keluaran. Beberapa masalah menghasilkan counts yang lebih merata (keadaan berentropi tinggi). Untuk circuit yang sangat dalam dengan banyak qubit, error gate dan derau yang tidak terkait error pengukuran sering kali menghasilkan keadaan berentropi tinggi. Dalam kasus seperti itu, M3 beroperasi pada ruang keadaan yang cukup besar. Ada tiga pendekatan yang mungkin untuk ini:\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Bullet: '(1) '
#, no-wrap
msgid "Accept that this is a limitation of M3.\n"
msgstr "Terima bahwa ini adalah keterbatasan M3.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Bullet: '(2) '
#, no-wrap
msgid "Note that the upper bound on the space of the matrix formed by M3 is `num_shots` $\\times$ `num_shots`, rather than $2^N \\times 2^N$, with the former being much smaller for large number of qubits $N$.\n"
msgstr "Perhatikan bahwa batas atas ruang matriks yang dibentuk M3 adalah `num_shots` $\\times$ `num_shots`, bukan $2^N \\times 2^N$, dengan yang pertama jauh lebih kecil untuk jumlah qubit $N$ yang besar.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Bullet: '(3) '
#, no-wrap
msgid "Only with great caution, remove very low-probability bitstrings from the raw counts fed into M3. This might introduce bias, and the limitation of the support of your states can interfere with convergence in approaches like the Krylov method. This should only be done when you know exactly why you are doing it and can anticipate all its effects. That is why such trimming is not a built-in feature of M3.\n"
msgstr "Hanya dengan sangat hati-hati, hapus bitstring berprobabilitas sangat rendah dari counts mentah yang diberikan ke M3. Ini bisa menimbulkan bias, dan pembatasan support keadaan Anda dapat mengganggu konvergensi pada pendekatan seperti metode Krylov. Ini hanya boleh dilakukan jika Anda tahu persis alasannya dan dapat mengantisipasi semua efeknya. Itulah sebabnya pemangkasan seperti ini bukan fitur bawaan M3.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "For more on the `mthree` Qiskit addon see examples, installation instructions, background, and API references in its [documentation](https://qiskit.github.io/qiskit-addon-mthree/).\n"
msgstr "Untuk info lebih lanjut tentang addon Qiskit `mthree`, lihat contoh, petunjuk instalasi, latar belakang, dan referensi API di [dokumentasinya](https://qiskit.github.io/qiskit-addon-mthree/).\n"

# doq: kept in English by the translator (name or code)
#. type: Title ##
#, no-wrap
msgid "Twirled readout error extinction (TREX) {#twirled-readout-error-extinction-trex}"
msgstr "Twirled readout error extinction (TREX) {#twirled-readout-error-extinction-trex}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Twirled Readout Error Extinction (TREX) is a measurement-error mitigation technique used on modern quantum computers. Its goal is to remove bias introduced by classical readout errors without requiring full characterization or inversion of the measurement assignment matrix.\n"
msgstr "Twirled Readout Error Extinction (TREX) adalah teknik measurement-error mitigation yang dipakai pada komputer kuantum modern. Tujuannya menghilangkan bias yang ditimbulkan error readout klasik tanpa memerlukan karakterisasi penuh atau inversi matriks assignment pengukuran.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "TREX is not error correction and does not improve state fidelity. Instead, it ensures that expectation value estimators are unbiased with respect to readout noise, up to statistical error, by averaging over randomized measurement bases and rescaling. On IBM Quantum hardware, TREX is the default mitigation method for expectation-value estimation and corresponds to `resilience_level = 1` in the IBM Quantum Estimator primitive.\n"
msgstr "TREX bukan error correction dan tidak meningkatkan fidelity keadaan. Sebaliknya, TREX memastikan estimator nilai ekspektasi tidak bias terhadap derau readout, hingga error statistik, dengan merata-ratakan basis pengukuran acak dan menskalakan ulang. Pada hardware IBM Quantum, TREX adalah metode mitigasi default untuk estimasi nilai ekspektasi dan bersesuaian dengan `resilience_level = 1` pada primitif IBM Quantum Estimator.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Nature of readout errors on superconducting hardware {#nature-of-readout-errors-on-superconducting-hardware}"
msgstr "Sifat error readout pada hardware superkonduktor {#nature-of-readout-errors-on-superconducting-hardware}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "On IBM superconducting qubits, readout errors are dominated by:\n"
msgstr "Pada qubit superkonduktor IBM, error readout didominasi oleh:\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, no-wrap
msgid "Asymmetric bit-flip probabilities (for example, $|1\\rangle \\rightarrow |0\\rangle$ more frequently than the reverse)\n"
msgstr "Probabilitas bit-flip asimetris (misalnya, $|1\\rangle \\rightarrow |0\\rangle$ lebih sering terjadi daripada sebaliknya)\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, no-wrap
msgid "State-dependent relaxation during measurement itself\n"
msgstr "Relaksasi yang bergantung pada keadaan selama pengukuran itu sendiri\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, no-wrap
msgid "Mild but non-negligible crosstalk between neighboring readout resonators\n"
msgstr "Crosstalk ringan tetapi tidak dapat diabaikan antara resonator readout yang bertetangga\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Importantly, these errors can often be modeled as a classical stochastic process acting on measurement outcomes. TREX exploits a standard idea from noise tailoring: randomization (twirling) of the measurement procedure. TREX typically proceeds in two stages:\n"
msgstr "Yang penting, error ini sering kali dapat dimodelkan sebagai proses stokastik klasik yang bekerja pada hasil pengukuran. TREX memanfaatkan gagasan standar dari noise tailoring: pengacakan (twirling) prosedur pengukuran. TREX biasanya berjalan dalam dua tahap:\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Bullet: '1. '
#, no-wrap
msgid "Twirling and classical \"untwirling\"\n"
msgstr "Twirling dan \"untwirling\" klasik\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Bullet: '2. '
#, no-wrap
msgid "Rescaling of expectation values using calibration circuits\n"
msgstr "Penskalaan ulang nilai ekspektasi memakai circuit kalibrasi\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Suppose an expectation value of observable $O$ is related to noisy estimates, like the following:\n"
msgstr "Misalkan nilai ekspektasi observable $O$ berhubungan dengan estimasi berderau, seperti berikut:\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\langle O \\rangle_\\text{ideal} \\approx a \\langle O \\rangle_\\text{meas}+b\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\langle O \\rangle_\\text{ideal} \\approx a \\langle O \\rangle_\\text{meas}+b\n"
"$$\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "where $a$ is a fixed factor that scales the estimate and $b$ is a bias from sources described above. We are using $\\approx$ here as a reminder that we are only addressing readout error here; there is still gate error to consider.\n"
msgstr "di mana $a$ adalah faktor tetap yang menskalakan estimasi dan $b$ adalah bias dari sumber-sumber yang dijelaskan di atas. Kita memakai $\\approx$ di sini sebagai pengingat bahwa kita hanya menangani error readout di sini; masih ada error gate yang perlu dipertimbangkan.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Just before measurement, each qubit is randomly conjugated with a Pauli operator (like I or X). Measurement outcomes are classically untwirled afterward.\n"
msgstr "Tepat sebelum pengukuran, setiap qubit dikonjugasi secara acak dengan operator Pauli (seperti I atau X). Hasil pengukuran kemudian di-untwirl secara klasik.\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![A diagram showing how Pauli gates are used in measurement twirling.](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/error-mitigation/trex-diagram.avif)\n"
msgstr "![A diagram showing how Pauli gates are used in measurement twirling.](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/error-mitigation/trex-diagram.avif)\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Averaging over these randomizations symmetrizes the readout channel. As a result, systematic asymmetries in the readout process are converted into a more symmetric error channel whose remaining effect can be treated through calibration and rescaling. In simple terms, if for example there was a bias toward the $|0\\rangle$ state, we would be missing some measurements of $|1\\rangle$. By twirling around the measurement, we randomly interchange which logical outcome is associated with the physical readout process, then undo that interchange classically afterward. Averaging over many such randomized measurements removes the systematic preference for one reported outcome over the other. This removes the dominant measurement bias under the assumptions of the TREX model, so that we have:\n"
msgstr "Merata-ratakan pengacakan ini membuat kanal readout menjadi simetris. Akibatnya, asimetri sistematis dalam proses readout diubah menjadi kanal error yang lebih simetris yang efek sisanya dapat ditangani melalui kalibrasi dan penskalaan ulang. Singkatnya, jika misalnya ada bias ke keadaan $|0\\rangle$, kita akan kehilangan sebagian pengukuran $|1\\rangle$. Dengan melakukan twirling di sekitar pengukuran, kita menukar secara acak hasil logis mana yang dikaitkan dengan proses readout fisik, lalu membatalkan pertukaran itu secara klasik setelahnya. Merata-ratakan banyak pengukuran acak seperti itu menghilangkan preferensi sistematis pada satu hasil yang dilaporkan dibanding yang lain. Ini menghilangkan bias pengukuran dominan di bawah asumsi model TREX, sehingga kita punya:\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\langle O \\rangle_\\text{ideal} \\approx a \\langle O \\rangle_\\text{twirled}+0\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\langle O \\rangle_\\text{ideal} \\approx a \\langle O \\rangle_\\text{twirled}+0\n"
"$$\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "However, expectation values might still need to be rescaled. The second stage is using calibration circuits (circuits with known outcomes) to estimate the scaling factor $a$. This can then be used to obtain a greatly enhanced estimate.\n"
msgstr "Namun, nilai ekspektasi mungkin masih perlu diskalakan ulang. Tahap kedua memakai circuit kalibrasi (circuit dengan hasil yang diketahui) untuk mengestimasi faktor skala $a$. Ini kemudian dapat dipakai untuk mendapatkan estimasi yang jauh lebih baik.\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\langle O \\rangle_\\text{ideal} \\approx \\langle O \\rangle_\\text{TREX} = a \\langle O \\rangle_\\text{twirled}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\langle O \\rangle_\\text{ideal} \\approx \\langle O \\rangle_\\text{TREX} = a \\langle O \\rangle_\\text{twirled}\n"
"$$\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Expectation values of Pauli observables become unbiased estimators of their ideal values. No explicit noise model is learned or inverted, which is why TREX is numerically stable and inexpensive. Again, gate error has not been addressed here.\n"
msgstr "Nilai ekspektasi observable Pauli menjadi estimator tak bias dari nilai idealnya. Tidak ada model derau eksplisit yang dipelajari atau dibalik, itulah sebabnya TREX stabil secara numerik dan murah. Sekali lagi, error gate belum ditangani di sini.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "In the estimation of an expectation value $\\langle O\\rangle$ of observable $O$, there can be variance due to finite sampling, there can be many types of incoherent and coherent errors, and there can also be a bias, $\\delta$, due to readout error. This bias arises from sources like the asymmetric transitions we've discussed. In practice, this bias is typically well-modeled as a classical stochastic channel acting after the quantum evolution. TREX transforms the effective measurement channel so that the estimator of $\\langle O\\rangle$ is unbiased with respect to readout error. This is relatively easy to see, using the simplest example of twirling gates: X. If there is asymmetry in $|1\\rangle \\rightarrow |0\\rangle$ compared to $|0\\rangle \\rightarrow |1\\rangle$, but we randomly switch which state is being read (flipping back later, classically), then this obviously removes the systematic asymmetry (though finite sampling still means there might be slight stochastic asymmetry in measurement). In practice, twirling gates for TREX are not limited to I and X, but this suffices for an explanation.\n"
msgstr "Dalam estimasi nilai ekspektasi $\\langle O\\rangle$ dari observable $O$, bisa ada varians akibat sampling terbatas, bisa ada banyak jenis error inkoheren dan koheren, dan bisa juga ada bias, $\\delta$, akibat error readout. Bias ini muncul dari sumber seperti transisi asimetris yang telah kita bahas. Dalam praktik, bias ini biasanya dimodelkan dengan baik sebagai kanal stokastik klasik yang bekerja setelah evolusi kuantum. TREX mengubah kanal pengukuran efektif sehingga estimator $\\langle O\\rangle$ tidak bias terhadap error readout. Ini relatif mudah dilihat dengan contoh twirling gate paling sederhana: X. Jika ada asimetri pada $|1\\rangle \\rightarrow |0\\rangle$ dibandingkan $|0\\rangle \\rightarrow |1\\rangle$, tetapi kita secara acak menukar keadaan mana yang dibaca (dan membaliknya kembali nanti secara klasik), maka jelas ini menghilangkan asimetri sistematis (meski sampling terbatas tetap berarti mungkin ada sedikit asimetri stokastik dalam pengukuran). Dalam praktik, gate twirling untuk TREX tidak terbatas pada I dan X, tetapi ini cukup untuk penjelasan.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "TREX allows us to obtain an estimate of an expectation value that is free of bias due to readout error. There could still be a bias from some other source (like coherent over-rotation); TREX targets only readout. Because TREX replaces systematic bias with randomized symmetrization, it generally converts bias into additional stochastic variance, increasing the variance of the estimator at fixed sampling cost.\n"
msgstr "TREX memungkinkan kita memperoleh estimasi nilai ekspektasi yang bebas dari bias akibat error readout. Masih bisa ada bias dari sumber lain (seperti over-rotation koheren); TREX hanya menargetkan readout. Karena TREX mengganti bias sistematis dengan simetrisasi acak, ia umumnya mengubah bias menjadi varians stokastik tambahan, sehingga meningkatkan varians estimator pada biaya sampling yang tetap.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Check your understanding {#check-your-understanding}"
msgstr "Periksa pemahaman Anda {#check-your-understanding}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Suppose we use estimator on a circuit that has prepared the state $|\\psi\\rangle = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(|0\\rangle+|1\\rangle)$ in the estimation of $\\langle Z\\rangle$. Suppose further that measurement errors cause outcomes corresponding to $|1\\rangle$ to be reported as $0$ 10% of the time, while outcomes corresponding to $|0\\rangle$ are reported as $1$ only 1% of the time. The asymmetry between these reporting errors introduces a measurement bias.\n"
msgstr "Misalkan kita memakai estimator pada circuit yang telah menyiapkan keadaan $|\\psi\\rangle = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(|0\\rangle+|1\\rangle)$ untuk mengestimasi $\\langle Z\\rangle$. Misalkan juga error pengukuran menyebabkan hasil yang bersesuaian dengan $|1\\rangle$ dilaporkan sebagai $0$ sebanyak 10% dari waktu, sedangkan hasil yang bersesuaian dengan $|0\\rangle$ dilaporkan sebagai $1$ hanya 1% dari waktu. Asimetri antara error pelaporan ini menimbulkan bias pengukuran.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "(a) In the absence of any such measurement errors, what would be the ideal expectation value of the observable $\\langle Z \\rangle$?\n"
msgstr "(a) Tanpa error pengukuran semacam itu, berapa nilai ekspektasi ideal dari observable $\\langle Z \\rangle$?\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "(b) With the asymmetric measurement or readout errors, what is the expectation value of the observable $\\langle Z \\rangle$?\n"
msgstr "(b) Dengan error pengukuran atau readout asimetris, berapa nilai ekspektasi observable $\\langle Z \\rangle$?\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "(c) Consider a limited set of Pauli twirling gates: I and X. Assume I and X are applied randomly with 50% probability in multiple shots. The same measurement error rates still apply. Then the Pauli gate (I or X) is undone classically. Make a table of all possible outcomes and their probabilities due to initial superposition, asymmetric measurement errors, and randomly assigned Pauli gates.\n"
msgstr "(c) Pertimbangkan himpunan terbatas gate Pauli twirling: I dan X. Anggap I dan X diterapkan secara acak dengan probabilitas 50% pada banyak shot. Laju error pengukuran yang sama tetap berlaku. Lalu gate Pauli (I atau X) dibatalkan secara klasik. Buat tabel semua hasil yang mungkin beserta probabilitasnya akibat superposisi awal, error pengukuran asimetris, dan gate Pauli yang ditetapkan secara acak.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "(d) Use the table to determine the expectation value $\\langle Z \\rangle$ using the twirling strategy.\n"
msgstr "(d) Gunakan tabel untuk menentukan nilai ekspektasi $\\langle Z \\rangle$ dengan strategi twirling.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "(e) Compare the expectation values: ideal, without twirling, and with twirling.\n"
msgstr "(e) Bandingkan nilai ekspektasi: ideal, tanpa twirling, dan dengan twirling.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "(f) What has been omitted from this treatment that is found in a full TREX application?\n"
msgstr "(f) Apa yang dihilangkan dari pembahasan ini yang ada dalam penerapan TREX penuh?\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Accordion>\n"
"<AccordionItem title=\"Answer\">\n"
msgstr ""
"<Accordion>\n"
"<AccordionItem title=\"Jawaban\">\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "(a) You can simply use intuition and symmetry to say $\\langle Z \\rangle = 0$. To show explicitly:\n"
msgstr "(a) Anda cukup memakai intuisi dan simetri untuk menyatakan $\\langle Z \\rangle = 0$. Untuk menunjukkannya secara eksplisit:\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\begin{aligned}\n"
"\\langle Z \\rangle & = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(\\langle0|+\\langle1|) Z \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(|0\\rangle+|1\\rangle)\\\\\n"
"& = \\frac{1}{2}(\\langle0|Z|0\\rangle+\\langle0|Z|1\\rangle+\\langle1|Z|0\\rangle+\\langle1|Z|1\\rangle)\\\\\n"
"& = \\frac{1}{2}((1)\\langle0|0\\rangle+(-1)\\langle0|1\\rangle+(1)\\langle1|0\\rangle+(-1)\\langle1|1\\rangle)\\\\\n"
"& = \\frac{1}{2}(1+0+0+-1)\\\\\n"
"& = 0\n"
"\\end{aligned}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\begin{aligned}\n"
"\\langle Z \\rangle & = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(\\langle0|+\\langle1|) Z \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(|0\\rangle+|1\\rangle)\\\\\n"
"& = \\frac{1}{2}(\\langle0|Z|0\\rangle+\\langle0|Z|1\\rangle+\\langle1|Z|0\\rangle+\\langle1|Z|1\\rangle)\\\\\n"
"& = \\frac{1}{2}((1)\\langle0|0\\rangle+(-1)\\langle0|1\\rangle+(1)\\langle1|0\\rangle+(-1)\\langle1|1\\rangle)\\\\\n"
"& = \\frac{1}{2}(1+0+0+-1)\\\\\n"
"& = 0\n"
"\\end{aligned}\n"
"$$\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"(b) The state could collapse to either $|0\\rangle$ or $|1\\rangle$ when measured, each with 50% chance. But of the 50% that collapse to the $|0\\rangle$ state, 1% of those (or 0.5% of all cases) will be mis-reported as $|1\\rangle$. Of the 50% that collapse to $|1\\rangle$, 10% (or 5% of all cases) will be mis-reported as $|0\\rangle$. This leaves us with:\n"
"* $|0\\rangle$ 54.5%\n"
"* $|1\\rangle$ 45.5%\n"
"Ignoring all other types of possible errors, we would obtain an estimate of\n"
msgstr ""
"(b) Keadaan bisa kolaps ke $|0\\rangle$ atau $|1\\rangle$ saat diukur, masing-masing dengan peluang 50%. Tetapi dari 50% yang kolaps ke keadaan $|0\\rangle$, 1% di antaranya (atau 0,5% dari semua kasus) akan salah dilaporkan sebagai $|1\\rangle$. Dari 50% yang kolaps ke $|1\\rangle$, 10% (atau 5% dari semua kasus) akan salah dilaporkan sebagai $|0\\rangle$. Ini menyisakan:\n"
"* $|0\\rangle$ 54,5%\n"
"* $|1\\rangle$ 45,5%\n"
"Dengan mengabaikan semua jenis error lain yang mungkin, kita akan memperoleh estimasi\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\langle Z \\rangle = (0.545)(1)+(0.455)(-1) = 0.09\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\langle Z \\rangle = (0.545)(1)+(0.455)(-1) = 0.09\n"
"$$\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "(c)\n"
msgstr "(c)\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"| Pauli | Ideal measurement outcome | Outcome after Pauli | Readout error | Undo Pauli | Final reported outcome | Probability |\n"
"|--------------|---------|----------|-------------|------------|------------|------------|\n"
"| I | 0 | 0 | None | I | 0 | 50\\% $\\times$ 50\\% $\\times$ 99% = 24.75\\% |\n"
"| I | 0 | 0 | $0 \\rightarrow 1$ | I | 1 | 50\\% $\\times$ 50\\% $\\times$ 1% = 0.25\\% |\n"
"| I | 1 | 1 | None | I | 1 | 50\\% $\\times$ 50\\% $\\times$ 90% = 22.5\\% |\n"
"| I | 1 | 1 | $1 \\rightarrow 0$ | I | 0 | 50\\% $\\times$ 50\\% $\\times$ 10% = 2.5\\% |\n"
"| X | 0 | 1 | None | X | 0 | 50\\% $\\times$ 50\\% $\\times$ 90% = 22.5\\% |\n"
"| X | 0 | 1 | $1 \\rightarrow 0$ | X | 1 | 50\\% $\\times$ 50\\% $\\times$ 10% = 2.5\\% |\n"
"| X | 1 | 0 | None | X | 1 | 50\\% $\\times$ 50\\% $\\times$ 99% = 24.75\\% |\n"
"| X | 1 | 0 | $0 \\rightarrow 1$ | X | 0 | 50\\% $\\times$ 50\\% $\\times$ 1% = 0.25\\% |\n"
msgstr ""
"| Pauli | Hasil pengukuran ideal | Hasil setelah Pauli | Error readout | Batalkan Pauli | Hasil akhir yang dilaporkan | Probabilitas |\n"
"|--------------|---------|----------|-------------|------------|------------|------------|\n"
"| I | 0 | 0 | Tidak ada | I | 0 | 50\\% $\\times$ 50\\% $\\times$ 99% = 24.75\\% |\n"
"| I | 0 | 0 | $0 \\rightarrow 1$ | I | 1 | 50\\% $\\times$ 50\\% $\\times$ 1% = 0.25\\% |\n"
"| I | 1 | 1 | Tidak ada | I | 1 | 50\\% $\\times$ 50\\% $\\times$ 90% = 22.5\\% |\n"
"| I | 1 | 1 | $1 \\rightarrow 0$ | I | 0 | 50\\% $\\times$ 50\\% $\\times$ 10% = 2.5\\% |\n"
"| X | 0 | 1 | Tidak ada | X | 0 | 50\\% $\\times$ 50\\% $\\times$ 90% = 22.5\\% |\n"
"| X | 0 | 1 | $1 \\rightarrow 0$ | X | 1 | 50\\% $\\times$ 50\\% $\\times$ 10% = 2.5\\% |\n"
"| X | 1 | 0 | Tidak ada | X | 1 | 50\\% $\\times$ 50\\% $\\times$ 99% = 24.75\\% |\n"
"| X | 1 | 0 | $0 \\rightarrow 1$ | X | 0 | 50\\% $\\times$ 50\\% $\\times$ 1% = 0.25\\% |\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "(d) From previous parts, we know the expectation values of Z in each of the final states. We need only take the weighted average:\n"
msgstr "(d) Dari bagian sebelumnya, kita tahu nilai ekspektasi Z pada masing-masing keadaan akhir. Kita tinggal mengambil rata-rata berbobot:\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\begin{aligned}\n"
"\\langle Z \\rangle & = (0.2475)(1)+(0.0025)(-1)+(0.225)(-1)+(0.025)(1)\\\\\n"
"& + (0.225)(1)+(0.025)(-1)+(0.2475)(-1)+(0.0025)(1)\\\\\n"
"& = 0\n"
"\\end{aligned}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\begin{aligned}\n"
"\\langle Z \\rangle & = (0.2475)(1)+(0.0025)(-1)+(0.225)(-1)+(0.025)(1)\\\\\n"
"& + (0.225)(1)+(0.025)(-1)+(0.2475)(-1)+(0.0025)(1)\\\\\n"
"& = 0\n"
"\\end{aligned}\n"
"$$\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "(e) In this very simple case our application of twirling recovered exactly the ideal expectation value. That is not typical. What is typical is that the twirling undoes biases like those introduced through asymmetric transitions, and returns an expectation value closer to the ideal.\n"
msgstr "(e) Dalam kasus yang sangat sederhana ini, penerapan twirling kita memulihkan persis nilai ekspektasi ideal. Itu tidak lazim. Yang lazim adalah twirling membatalkan bias seperti yang ditimbulkan oleh transisi asimetris, dan menghasilkan nilai ekspektasi yang lebih dekat ke nilai ideal.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "(f) This is a toy model application. There could be several things missing. Notably, we have not run calibration circuits here to estimate a rescaling factor $a$. This is typically necessary.\n"
msgstr "(f) Ini adalah penerapan model sederhana. Bisa ada beberapa hal yang hilang. Terutama, kita belum menjalankan circuit kalibrasi di sini untuk mengestimasi faktor penskalaan ulang $a$. Ini biasanya diperlukan.\n"

