# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, no-wrap
msgid "Measurement\n"
msgstr "Measurement\n"

# doq: fixed after review 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The effective inverse noise map is realized statistically in classical post-processing. This allows for more general inversion of noise channels that would otherwise have required non-physical steps like negative probabilities.\n"
msgstr "Peta noise invers yang efektif direalisasikan secara statistik dalam pasca-pemrosesan klasik. Ini memungkinkan inversi yang lebih umum untuk kanal noise yang tanpa cara ini akan membutuhkan langkah non-fisik seperti probabilitas negatif.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The price we pay for this is variance.\n"
"Each shot formerly had a \"magnitude\" equal to the possible measurement outcome magnitudes $|\\pm 1| = 1$. After scaling by the weight factor $W$, it has magnitude $W$ (1.375 in our example above).\n"
"$$\n"
"\\text{variance} \\propto W^2 \\approx 1.89\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"Harga yang harus kita bayar untuk ini adalah varians.\n"
"Setiap shot sebelumnya punya \"magnitudo\" yang sama dengan magnitudo hasil pengukuran yang mungkin, yaitu $|\\pm 1| = 1$. Setelah diskalakan dengan faktor bobot $W$, magnitudonya menjadi $W$ (1.375 pada contoh di atas).\n"
"$$\n"
"\\text{variance} \\propto W^2 \\approx 1.89\n"
"$$\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "That is the origin of the sampling overhead of PEC.\n"
msgstr "Itulah asal-usul sampling overhead pada PEC.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Built-in PEC {#built-in-pec}"
msgstr "PEC bawaan {#built-in-pec}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Now we will use PEC as it is built into IBM Quantum primitives. It is critical here to distinguish between full gate-level PEC and a local noise learning model for more scalable PEC, which is used by IBM Quantum primitives. The overhead of using full PEC would would restrict our application of it to small circuits. That's fine, but it is more useful to have a tool that can scale to a size at which quantum computing becomes valuable for real-world problems. For this reason, IBM Quantum primtives use a layer-wise PEC using a learned Pauli-Lindblad noise model, with aggressive scalability safeguards. In this implementation of PEC, noise is learned per repeated circuit layer, not per gate instance, and the noise model is Pauli-twirled, local, and Markovian. Clifford gates are absorbed into the noise model, while non-Clifford gates dominate the mitigation cost. Sampling overhead is capped to avoid exponential blow-ups.\n"
msgstr "Sekarang kita akan memakai PEC seperti yang sudah tertanam di primitif IBM Quantum. Di sini penting untuk membedakan antara PEC penuh di level gate dan model pembelajaran noise lokal untuk PEC yang lebih scalable, yang dipakai oleh primitif IBM Quantum. Overhead dari PEC penuh akan membatasi penerapannya hanya pada circuit kecil. Itu tidak masalah, tetapi lebih berguna punya alat yang bisa diskalakan ke ukuran di mana komputasi kuantum menjadi bernilai untuk masalah dunia nyata. Karena itu, primitif IBM Quantum memakai PEC per layer dengan model noise Pauli-Lindblad yang dipelajari, lengkap dengan pengaman skalabilitas yang agresif. Dalam implementasi PEC ini, noise dipelajari per layer circuit yang berulang, bukan per instans gate, dan model noise-nya di-Pauli-twirl, lokal, dan Markovian. Gate Clifford diserap ke dalam model noise, sementara gate non-Clifford mendominasi biaya mitigasi. Sampling overhead dibatasi agar tidak terjadi ledakan eksponensial.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "We will apply the PEC built in to IBM Quantum primitives at two scales: small (six qubits) and mid-sized (30 qubits) to see how cost is kept management in this version of PEC. Within each size experiment, we will also explicitly compare the results from PEC to those with no mitigation, with ZNE, and to the ideal value.\n"
msgstr "Kita akan menerapkan PEC bawaan primitif IBM Quantum pada dua skala: kecil (enam qubit) dan menengah (30 qubit) untuk melihat bagaimana biaya tetap terkendali dalam versi PEC ini. Pada setiap ukuran eksperimen, kita juga akan membandingkan secara eksplisit hasil PEC dengan hasil tanpa mitigasi, dengan ZNE, dan dengan nilai ideal.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "We start by importing a few necessary packages.\n"
msgstr "Kita mulai dengan mengimpor beberapa paket yang diperlukan.\n"

# doq: fixed after review 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "We choose an observable to estimate. The observable below is the Hamiltonian of a chain of Ising-like spins with nearest-neighbor coupling only in the $X$ component of their interaction, and a single-qubit term in $Z$, as though due to an external field in $Z$.\n"
msgstr "Kita memilih observable untuk diestimasi. Observable di bawah adalah Hamiltonian dari rantai spin mirip Ising dengan kopling tetangga terdekat hanya pada komponen $X$ dari interaksinya, dan suku satu qubit pada $Z$, seolah-olah akibat medan eksternal pada $Z$.\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\mathcal{H} = J_X\\sum_i{X_iX_{i+1}}+B\\sum_i{Z_i}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\mathcal{H} = J_X\\sum_i{X_iX_{i+1}}+B\\sum_i{Z_i}\n"
"$$\n"

# doq: fixed after review 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "There is no special reason for the selection of this Hamiltonian. We simply need a combination of an observable and circuit that is non-trivial so that error mitigation can do its work. We explicitly scale the Hamiltonian (using the coefficients), dividing out the number of qubits so that the energy will remain at the same order of magnitude as we increase the number of qubits. We begin with only six.\n"
msgstr "Tidak ada alasan khusus untuk memilih Hamiltonian ini. Kita hanya butuh kombinasi observable dan circuit yang tidak trivial supaya mitigasi error bisa bekerja. Kita menskalakan Hamiltonian secara eksplisit (memakai koefisien), dengan membagi jumlah qubit, sehingga energinya tetap berada di orde besaran yang sama saat jumlah qubit bertambah. Kita mulai dengan hanya enam.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "As in the choice of Hamiltonian, the circuit for state preparation below is not chosen for any physical reasons. Rather, it is chosen to have sufficient two-qubit depth and sufficiently many non-Clifford gates that PEC can be useful. There is no reason to think that this circuit is a good approximation of the ground state of this chain of spins, for example.\n"
msgstr "Seperti pada pilihan Hamiltonian, circuit untuk persiapan state di bawah tidak dipilih karena alasan fisika tertentu. Circuit ini dipilih supaya punya kedalaman dua qubit yang cukup dan gate non-Clifford yang cukup banyak sehingga PEC bisa berguna. Tidak ada alasan untuk mengira circuit ini adalah aproksimasi yang baik dari ground state rantai spin ini, misalnya.\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/error-mitigation/extracted-outputs/d61a4174-0.avif)\n"
msgstr "![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/error-mitigation/extracted-outputs/d61a4174-0.avif)\n"

# doq: fixed after review 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "We transpile our circuit and apply the resulting layout to our observables.\n"
msgstr "Kita men-transpile circuit kita dan menerapkan layout hasilnya ke observable kita.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "We obtain the ideal expectation value using the Statevector simulator.\n"
msgstr "Kita memperoleh nilai ekspektasi ideal memakai simulator Statevector.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "We expect that other ideal values will be similar in size. Now we run this on a real IBM quantum computer with no error mitigation.\n"
msgstr "Kita perkirakan nilai ideal lainnya akan berukuran mirip. Sekarang kita jalankan ini di komputer kuantum IBM sungguhan tanpa mitigasi error.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Now we apply ZNE to the same problem.\n"
msgstr "Sekarang kita terapkan ZNE pada masalah yang sama.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Finally, we apply PEC.\n"
msgstr "Terakhir, kita terapkan PEC.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Below we plot our results and compare.\n"
msgstr "Di bawah ini kita plot hasilnya dan membandingkannya.\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/error-mitigation/extracted-outputs/626e82d3-0.avif)\n"
msgstr "![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/error-mitigation/extracted-outputs/626e82d3-0.avif)\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "This worked very well. Our estimate using PEC is just 0.2% from the ideal value.\n"
msgstr "Ini bekerja dengan sangat baik. Estimasi kita memakai PEC hanya berbeda 0.2% dari nilai ideal.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "But remember how the quantum overhead grows exponentially. We enforced a maximum overhead of `max_overhead = 100`. From the time and the quality of the results, it looks like we might not have hit that maximum. However, if we scale up our calculation and leave that maximum in place, we will hit it, and our results will suffer.\n"
msgstr "Tapi ingat bahwa overhead kuantum tumbuh secara eksponensial. Kita menetapkan overhead maksimum `max_overhead = 100`. Dari waktu dan kualitas hasilnya, sepertinya kita mungkin belum mencapai maksimum itu. Namun, kalau kita memperbesar skala perhitungan dan tetap memakai batas maksimum itu, kita akan mencapainya, dan hasil kita akan menurun.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"We can see what happens when we scale our circuit up to 30 qubits. Interestingly, on many laptops the Statevector simulator will start to time out around 24-28 qubits. We could find computational ways around this. But instead, let's embrace the fact that quantum computers will be able to solve some problems at a scale at which classical computers cannot keep up. That is the point. What can we do, then, to validate our quantum computing results in those cases? There are several potential answers:\n"
"* Some problems are difficult to solve but easy to check (like prime factoring as in Shor's algorithm).\n"
"* Some problems have parameter regimes or flavors of the problem that reduce in complexity and can be solved classically, or even analytically, to compare a few points.\n"
"In this case, we chose a Hamiltonian that is intrinsic (we divided out the number of qubits). The ideal expectation value should be fairly stable. Let's see how the ideal expectation value varies with the number of qubits and see if we can make classical predictions about the value at 30 qubits.\n"
msgstr ""
"Kita bisa melihat apa yang terjadi saat circuit kita diperbesar sampai 30 qubit. Menariknya, di banyak laptop simulator Statevector akan mulai mengalami timeout sekitar 24-28 qubit. Kita bisa mencari cara komputasi untuk mengatasinya. Tapi sebaliknya, mari kita terima kenyataan bahwa komputer kuantum akan mampu menyelesaikan beberapa masalah pada skala yang tidak bisa diikuti komputer klasik. Itulah intinya. Lalu, apa yang bisa kita lakukan untuk memvalidasi hasil komputasi kuantum kita dalam kasus seperti itu? Ada beberapa jawaban yang mungkin:\n"
"* Beberapa masalah sulit diselesaikan tetapi mudah diperiksa (seperti faktorisasi prima pada algoritma Shor).\n"
"* Beberapa masalah punya rezim parameter atau varian masalah yang kompleksitasnya menurun dan bisa diselesaikan secara klasik, atau bahkan secara analitik, untuk membandingkan beberapa titik.\n"
"Dalam kasus ini, kita memilih Hamiltonian yang intrinsik (kita membagi dengan jumlah qubit). Nilai ekspektasi idealnya seharusnya cukup stabil. Mari kita lihat bagaimana nilai ekspektasi ideal berubah terhadap jumlah qubit dan apakah kita bisa membuat prediksi klasik untuk nilai pada 30 qubit.\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/error-mitigation/extracted-outputs/e5d5f7f5-1.avif)\n"
msgstr "![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/error-mitigation/extracted-outputs/e5d5f7f5-1.avif)\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Indeed, we see that we have selected a Hamiltonian and state that does not change with the number of qubits. This is a very poor use for a research application of quantum computing, but an excellent one for benchmarking error mitigation techniques. We can confidently use this fixed expectation value as the ideal result, even as we scale up the number of qubits for the testing of error mitigation.\n"
msgstr "Memang, kita melihat bahwa kita telah memilih Hamiltonian dan state yang tidak berubah seiring jumlah qubit. Ini pilihan yang sangat buruk untuk aplikasi riset komputasi kuantum, tetapi sangat bagus untuk benchmarking teknik mitigasi error. Kita bisa dengan yakin memakai nilai ekspektasi tetap ini sebagai hasil ideal, bahkan saat kita menambah jumlah qubit untuk menguji mitigasi error.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Now we run this larger circuit with ZNE.\n"
msgstr "Sekarang kita jalankan circuit yang lebih besar ini dengan ZNE.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Finally, we run the 30-qubit example with PEC.\n"
msgstr "Terakhir, kita jalankan contoh 30 qubit dengan PEC.\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/error-mitigation/extracted-outputs/d4e98091-0.avif)\n"
msgstr "![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/error-mitigation/extracted-outputs/d4e98091-0.avif)\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Not only did PEC take much longer than the other options, but we clearly reached our maximum overhead threshold. The PEC results paid the overhead price, but the overhead cap prevented sufficiently accurate cancellation of the learned noise model. This is critical. At six qubits, we were able to leverage PEC and get better results with only a modest increase in quantum overhead (about a factor of 5). At 30 qubits, we paid a much higher price (about a factor of 60 times higher) and the results were even worse than the unmitigated estimate. This is not to say that PEC can never work with 30 qubits; that might be circuit-dependent. The message is simply that the exponential growth in quantum overhead makes it difficult to run for large circuits.\n"
msgstr "PEC tidak hanya memakan waktu jauh lebih lama daripada opsi lain, tetapi kita jelas mencapai ambang overhead maksimum kita. Hasil PEC sudah membayar harga overhead, tetapi batas overhead mencegah pembatalan model noise yang dipelajari secara cukup akurat. Ini penting. Pada enam qubit, kita bisa memanfaatkan PEC dan mendapat hasil lebih baik dengan peningkatan overhead kuantum yang hanya sedang (sekitar faktor 5). Pada 30 qubit, kita membayar harga yang jauh lebih tinggi (sekitar 60 kali lebih tinggi) dan hasilnya malah lebih buruk daripada estimasi tanpa mitigasi. Ini bukan berarti PEC tidak pernah bisa bekerja dengan 30 qubit; itu mungkin bergantung pada circuit-nya. Pesannya sederhana: pertumbuhan eksponensial overhead kuantum membuatnya sulit dijalankan untuk circuit besar.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Key takeaways: {#key-takeaways}"
msgstr "Poin-poin penting: {#key-takeaways}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, no-wrap
msgid "Measurement mitigation often helps and usually carries low overhead: it removes a bias that does not scale with circuit depth and it composes cleanly with nearly everything.\n"
msgstr "Mitigasi pengukuran sering membantu dan biasanya beroverhead rendah: ia menghilangkan bias yang tidak membesar seiring kedalaman circuit dan bisa digabungkan dengan mulus dengan hampir semua teknik lain.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, no-wrap
msgid "Some techniques cannot be combined. Even when they can be, make sure you combine them in a consistent way such that one is not undoing the other. A good example is ensuring that twirling is on during the calibration for M3 if those methods are to be combined.\n"
msgstr "Beberapa teknik tidak bisa digabungkan. Bahkan ketika bisa, pastikan kamu menggabungkannya secara konsisten sehingga yang satu tidak membatalkan yang lain. Contoh yang baik adalah memastikan twirling aktif selama kalibrasi untuk M3 jika metode-metode itu akan digabungkan.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, no-wrap
msgid "PEC and ZNE should be thought of as alternatives to each other. They solve the same problem with opposite tradeoffs:\n"
msgstr "PEC dan ZNE sebaiknya dipandang sebagai alternatif satu sama lain. Keduanya menyelesaikan masalah yang sama dengan trade-off yang berlawanan:\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Bullet: '  - '
#, no-wrap
msgid "PEC: yields unbiased expectation values at the cost of exponential variance.\n"
msgstr "PEC: menghasilkan nilai ekspektasi tanpa bias dengan biaya varians eksponensial.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Bullet: '  - '
#, no-wrap
msgid "ZNE generally yields residual bias that depends on the extrapolation model, but at moderate cost.\n"
msgstr "ZNE umumnya menghasilkan bias residual yang bergantung pada model ekstrapolasi, tetapi dengan biaya yang moderat.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, no-wrap
msgid "DD can help with long idle periods, but it reshapes the noise model and can invalidate extrapolation or inversion assumptions.\n"
msgstr "DD bisa membantu pada periode idle yang panjang, tetapi ia membentuk ulang model noise dan bisa membatalkan asumsi ekstrapolasi atau inversi.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, no-wrap
msgid "Pauli twirling does not reduce noise. It reshapes it and often enables other techniques to work better (ZNE and PEC).\n"
msgstr "Pauli twirling tidak mengurangi noise. Ia membentuk ulang noise dan sering membuat teknik lain bekerja lebih baik (ZNE dan PEC).\n"
